            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 1: FAMILY LIFE</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying sharing household chores and strengthening family bonds have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When family members see their close friends willingly engage in household chores, they are far more likely to join in and teach children responsibility and teamwork.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to divide housework fairly at home or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school reduce stress on working mothers and strengthen family bonds.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 1: Family Life)</i>"
