            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 1: FAMILY LIFE</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that sharing household chores and strengthening family bonds is important, yet still find it difficult to divide housework fairly at home every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, family members can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on household chores. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners teach children responsibility and teamwork without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts reduce stress on working mothers and strengthen family bonds. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 1: Family Life report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, household chores stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 1: Family Life)</i>"
