            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON HUMANS AND THE ENVIRONMENT</b>\nIn recent years, adopting a green lifestyle and reducing our carbon footprint has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of citizens and students recognise the importance of green habits, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>reduced</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in our community. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to adopt an eco-friendly lifestyle.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that cut down on single-use plastic waste and save energy, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how green habits can reduce our household carbon footprint and protect local ecosystems.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should adopt an eco-friendly lifestyle, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in our community. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting adopting a green lifestyle and reducing our carbon footprint matters for the long-term future. Young people who see citizens and students model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, green habits not only cut down on single-use plastic waste and save energy today but also reduce our household carbon footprint and protect local ecosystems for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 2: Humans and the Environment)</i>"
