            "explanation": "sustained = maintained (duy trì bền bỉ suốt cả năm học).",
            "passage": "<b>PARTNERSHIPS BETWEEN SCHOOLS AND FAMILIES IN UNIT 2: HUMANS AND THE ENVIRONMENT</b>\nOver the past two years, a research team from the National Institute of Education has tracked 1,500 high school students to evaluate how adopting a green lifestyle and reducing our carbon footprint affects teenager development. The study found that while nearly ninety per cent of citizens and students valued green habits, only forty-two per cent <u><b>sustained</b></u> these routines throughout the entire school year. Students who tried to practise alone often gave up after a few weeks when exam pressure increased.\nBy contrast, learners whose families and teachers coordinated their guidance achieved far more durable results. In households where parents actively joined weekend activities to adopt an eco-friendly lifestyle in our community, teenagers were twice as likely to cut down on single-use plastic waste and save energy. Researchers explained that shared routines reduce the feeling of isolation and turn abstract classroom advice into enjoyable daily habits. Moreover, consistent family involvement helps reduce our household carbon footprint and protect local ecosystems without adding extra stress to students’ timetables.\nSchools across the country are now redesigning their programmes based on these findings. <b>[I]</b> Instead of assigning theoretical worksheets, teachers invite parents and students to co-sign a simple weekly action plan. <b>[II]</b> This joint commitment ensures that classroom lessons are reinforced at home every evening. <b>[III]</b> At monthly review sessions, classes celebrate collective progress and exchange practical solutions to common obstacles. <b>[IV]</b>\nThe long-term implications of this partnership model are encouraging. When citizens and students work side by side, <u><b>they</b></u> build mutual respect and lasting life skills. Ultimately, integrating adopting a green lifestyle and reducing our carbon footprint into both school curricula and family life prepares young citizens to thrive in an increasingly complex world.\n<i>(Adapted from National Educational Research Reports – Unit 2: Humans and the Environment)</i>"
