            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 2: HUMANS AND THE ENVIRONMENT</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying adopting a green lifestyle and reducing our carbon footprint have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When citizens and students see their close friends willingly engage in green habits, they are far more likely to join in and cut down on single-use plastic waste and save energy.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to adopt an eco-friendly lifestyle in our community or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school reduce our household carbon footprint and protect local ecosystems.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 2: Humans and the Environment)</i>"
