            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 2: HUMANS AND THE ENVIRONMENT</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that adopting a green lifestyle and reducing our carbon footprint is important, yet still find it difficult to adopt an eco-friendly lifestyle in our community every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, citizens and students can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on green habits. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners cut down on single-use plastic waste and save energy without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts reduce our household carbon footprint and protect local ecosystems. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 2: Humans and the Environment report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, green habits stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 2: Humans and the Environment)</i>"
