            "passage": "<b>PARTNERSHIPS BETWEEN SCHOOLS AND FAMILIES IN UNIT 3: MUSIC</b>\nOver the past two years, a research team from the National Institute of Education has tracked 1,500 high school students to evaluate how enjoying traditional and modern music shows affects teenager development. The study found that while nearly ninety per cent of young musicians and audiences valued musical performances, only forty-two per cent <u><b>sustained</b></u> these routines throughout the entire school year. Students who tried to practise alone often gave up after a few weeks when exam pressure increased.\nBy contrast, learners whose families and teachers coordinated their guidance achieved far more durable results. In households where parents actively joined weekend activities to appreciate both folk music and modern pop on the concert stage, teenagers were twice as likely to connect people from different cultures and relieve daily stress. Researchers explained that shared routines reduce the feeling of isolation and turn abstract classroom advice into enjoyable daily habits. Moreover, consistent family involvement helps inspire teenagers to learn traditional musical instruments without adding extra stress to students’ timetables.\nSchools across the country are now redesigning their programmes based on these findings. <b>[I]</b> Instead of assigning theoretical worksheets, teachers invite parents and students to co-sign a simple weekly action plan. <b>[II]</b> This joint commitment ensures that classroom lessons are reinforced at home every evening. <b>[III]</b> At monthly review sessions, classes celebrate collective progress and exchange practical solutions to common obstacles. <b>[IV]</b>\nThe long-term implications of this partnership model are encouraging. When young musicians and audiences work side by side, <u><b>they</b></u> build mutual respect and lasting life skills. Ultimately, integrating enjoying traditional and modern music shows into both school curricula and family life prepares young citizens to thrive in an increasingly complex world.\n<i>(Adapted from National Educational Research Reports – Unit 3: Music)</i>"
