            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 3: MUSIC</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying enjoying traditional and modern music shows have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When young musicians and audiences see their close friends willingly engage in musical performances, they are far more likely to join in and connect people from different cultures and relieve daily stress.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to appreciate both folk music and modern pop on the concert stage or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school inspire teenagers to learn traditional musical instruments.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 3: Music)</i>"
