            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 3: MUSIC</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about enjoying traditional and modern music shows. Rather than treating Unit 3: Music as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to connect people from different cultures and relieve daily stress.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to appreciate both folk music and modern pop on the concert stage. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on musical performances. Through these authentic tasks, young musicians and audiences discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped inspire teenagers to learn traditional musical instruments. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making enjoying traditional and modern music shows an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 3: Music)</i>"
