            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 3: MUSIC</b>\nPractising musical performances is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying enjoying traditional and modern music shows have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to connect people from different cultures and relieve daily stress.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to appreciate both folk music and modern pop on the concert stage this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps young musicians and audiences inspire teenagers to learn traditional musical instruments with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 3: Music)</i>"
