            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON FOR A BETTER COMMUNITY</b>\nIn recent years, volunteering and helping disadvantaged people in the community has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of student volunteers recognise the importance of volunteer activities, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>donated</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in remote communities. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to take part in community service.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that support disadvantaged children and elderly neighbours, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how volunteer activities can build confidence, empathy and practical life skills for teenagers.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should take part in community service, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in remote communities. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting volunteering and helping disadvantaged people in the community matters for the long-term future. Young people who see student volunteers model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, volunteer activities not only support disadvantaged children and elderly neighbours today but also build confidence, empathy and practical life skills for teenagers for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 4: For a Better Community)</i>"
