            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 4: FOR A BETTER COMMUNITY</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying volunteering and helping disadvantaged people in the community have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When student volunteers see their close friends willingly engage in volunteer activities, they are far more likely to join in and support disadvantaged children and elderly neighbours.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to take part in community service in remote communities or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school build confidence, empathy and practical life skills for teenagers.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 4: For a Better Community)</i>"
