            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 4: FOR A BETTER COMMUNITY</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about volunteering and helping disadvantaged people in the community. Rather than treating Unit 4: For a Better Community as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to support disadvantaged children and elderly neighbours.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to take part in community service in remote communities. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on volunteer activities. Through these authentic tasks, student volunteers discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped build confidence, empathy and practical life skills for teenagers. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making volunteering and helping disadvantaged people in the community an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 4: For a Better Community)</i>"
