            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON INVENTIONS</b>\nIn recent years, modern inventions, smartphones and artificial intelligence has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of scientists and students recognise the importance of modern inventions, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>invented</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in classrooms and homes. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to use digital inventions wisely.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that save time on routine chores and store vast amounts of data, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how modern inventions can help students learn foreign languages interactively anytime.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should use digital inventions wisely, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in classrooms and homes. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting modern inventions, smartphones and artificial intelligence matters for the long-term future. Young people who see scientists and students model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, modern inventions not only save time on routine chores and store vast amounts of data today but also help students learn foreign languages interactively anytime for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 5: Inventions)</i>"
