            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 5: INVENTIONS</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying modern inventions, smartphones and artificial intelligence have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When scientists and students see their close friends willingly engage in modern inventions, they are far more likely to join in and save time on routine chores and store vast amounts of data.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to use digital inventions wisely in classrooms and homes or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school help students learn foreign languages interactively anytime.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 5: Inventions)</i>"
