            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 5: INVENTIONS</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about modern inventions, smartphones and artificial intelligence. Rather than treating Unit 5: Inventions as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to save time on routine chores and store vast amounts of data.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to use digital inventions wisely in classrooms and homes. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on modern inventions. Through these authentic tasks, scientists and students discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped help students learn foreign languages interactively anytime. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making modern inventions, smartphones and artificial intelligence an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 5: Inventions)</i>"
