            "passage": "<b>MAKING GENDER EQUALITY PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to promote equal rights and fair pay may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed career opportunities help men and women allow both men and women to pursue their dream careers. At the same time, these practices reduce poverty and boost economic growth for the whole society and foster stronger cooperation in the workplace.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in career opportunities has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>equal</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. Men and women can start with simple steps in the workplace, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind promoting gender equality in education and careers become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>opportunity</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 6: Gender Equality)</i>"
