            "passage": "<b>WHY CONSISTENCY MATTERS MORE THAN PERFECTION IN UNIT 6: GENDER EQUALITY</b>\nWhen people first decide to focus on promoting gender equality in education and careers, they often set overly ambitious targets. Enthusiastic beginners might promise to transform their entire lifestyle in a single weekend, only to feel exhausted a few days later. Studies on habit formation show that well-paced career opportunities are far more effective when built step by step, helping men and women allow both men and women to pursue their dream careers without burnout.\nThe main reason crash campaigns fail is that <u><b>they</b></u> demand too much willpower at once. <u><b>Small, consistent efforts practised every day produce far more lasting results than occasional bursts of intense activity.</b></u> When a routine fits naturally into a family’s or student’s daily schedule, it requires less mental strain to maintain.\nExperts therefore recommend a <u><b>gradual</b></u> approach to promote equal rights and fair pay in the workplace. By starting with one simple task each day and celebrating weekly milestones, learners steadily build momentum. Such steady practice not only strengthens personal discipline but also helps communities reduce poverty and boost economic growth for the whole society.\nOf course, occasional setbacks are normal during busy exam weeks or family events. Rather than abandoning the goal altogether, resilient learners simply resume their routine the next morning, knowing that patience will <u><b>foster</b></u> long-term success.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 6: Gender Equality)</i>"
