            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 6: GENDER EQUALITY</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying promoting gender equality in education and careers have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When men and women see their close friends willingly engage in career opportunities, they are far more likely to join in and allow both men and women to pursue their dream careers.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to promote equal rights and fair pay in the workplace or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school reduce poverty and boost economic growth for the whole society.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 6: Gender Equality)</i>"
