            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 6: GENDER EQUALITY</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that promoting gender equality in education and careers is important, yet still find it difficult to promote equal rights and fair pay in the workplace every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, men and women can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on career opportunities. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners allow both men and women to pursue their dream careers without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts reduce poverty and boost economic growth for the whole society. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 6: Gender Equality report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, career opportunities stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 6: Gender Equality)</i>"
