            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 6: GENDER EQUALITY</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about promoting gender equality in education and careers. Rather than treating Unit 6: Gender Equality as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to allow both men and women to pursue their dream careers.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to promote equal rights and fair pay in the workplace. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on career opportunities. Through these authentic tasks, men and women discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped reduce poverty and boost economic growth for the whole society. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making promoting gender equality in education and careers an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 6: Gender Equality)</i>"
