            "explanation": "Trong ngữ cảnh đoạn 1, 'joined' đồng nghĩa với 'entered'.",
            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON VIET NAM AND INTERNATIONAL ORGANISATIONS</b>\nIn recent years, Viet Nam's active participation in international organisations has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of member countries and youth delegates recognise the importance of international programmes, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>joined</b></u> these practices into their daily routines across Southeast Asia. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to cooperate closely with international organisations.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that improve healthcare and education for disadvantaged children, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how international programmes can promote trade, peacekeeping and cultural exchange worldwide.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should cooperate closely with international organisations, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role across Southeast Asia. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting Viet Nam's active participation in international organisations matters for the long-term future. Young people who see member countries and youth delegates model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, international programmes not only improve healthcare and education for disadvantaged children today but also promote trade, peacekeeping and cultural exchange worldwide for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 7: Viet Nam and International Organisations)</i>"
