            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 7: VIET NAM AND INTERNATIONAL ORGANISATIONS</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying Viet Nam's active participation in international organisations have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When member countries and youth delegates see their close friends willingly engage in international programmes, they are far more likely to join in and improve healthcare and education for disadvantaged children.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to cooperate closely with international organisations across Southeast Asia or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school promote trade, peacekeeping and cultural exchange worldwide.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 7: Viet Nam and International Organisations)</i>"
