            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 7: VIET NAM AND INTERNATIONAL ORGANISATIONS</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that Viet Nam's active participation in international organisations is important, yet still find it difficult to cooperate closely with international organisations across Southeast Asia every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, member countries and youth delegates can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on international programmes. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners improve healthcare and education for disadvantaged children without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts promote trade, peacekeeping and cultural exchange worldwide. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 7: Viet Nam and International Organisations report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, international programmes stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 7: Viet Nam and International Organisations)</i>"
