            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 7: VIET NAM AND INTERNATIONAL ORGANISATIONS</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about Viet Nam's active participation in international organisations. Rather than treating Unit 7: Viet Nam and International Organisations as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to improve healthcare and education for disadvantaged children.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to cooperate closely with international organisations across Southeast Asia. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on international programmes. Through these authentic tasks, member countries and youth delegates discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped promote trade, peacekeeping and cultural exchange worldwide. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making Viet Nam's active participation in international organisations an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 7: Viet Nam and International Organisations)</i>"
