            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON NEW WAYS TO LEARN</b>\nIn recent years, blended learning, online courses and digital tools in education has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of teachers and students recognise the importance of learning methods, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>combined</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in modern classrooms. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to combine traditional and digital learning.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that combine face-to-face discussions with flexible online study, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how learning methods can help students develop self-study, teamwork and digital research skills.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should combine traditional and digital learning, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in modern classrooms. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting blended learning, online courses and digital tools in education matters for the long-term future. Young people who see teachers and students model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, learning methods not only combine face-to-face discussions with flexible online study today but also help students develop self-study, teamwork and digital research skills for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 8: New Ways to Learn)</i>"
