            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 8: NEW WAYS TO LEARN</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying blended learning, online courses and digital tools in education have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When teachers and students see their close friends willingly engage in learning methods, they are far more likely to join in and combine face-to-face discussions with flexible online study.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to combine traditional and digital learning in modern classrooms or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school help students develop self-study, teamwork and digital research skills.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 8: New Ways to Learn)</i>"
