            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 8: NEW WAYS TO LEARN</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that blended learning, online courses and digital tools in education is important, yet still find it difficult to combine traditional and digital learning in modern classrooms every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, teachers and students can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on learning methods. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners combine face-to-face discussions with flexible online study without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts help students develop self-study, teamwork and digital research skills. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 8: New Ways to Learn report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, learning methods stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 8: New Ways to Learn)</i>"
