            "passage": "<b>COMBINING TRADITIONAL VALUES WITH MODERN TOOLS IN UNIT 8: NEW WAYS TO LEARN</b>\nIn the digital age, some commentators worry that modern technology might distract young people from blended learning, online courses and digital tools in education. Yet recent educational projects show that when used wisely, digital tools can actually strengthen learning methods. Shared mobile calendars, reminder apps and online group checklists make it easier than ever for teachers and students to combine face-to-face discussions with flexible online study.\nOf course, technology alone cannot solve every problem. Researchers warn against relying passively on apps while ignoring face-to-face communication, as <u><b>they</b></u> are only tools in the hands of users. <u><b>Digital tools should serve as a practical bridge to support daily cooperation rather than a replacement for genuine human interaction.</b></u>\nWhen families and classmates combine smart reminders with warm personal encouragement, they can <u><b>preserve</b></u> the core spirit of Unit 8: New Ways to Learn. For example, a quick evening message can remind everyone to combine traditional and digital learning in modern classrooms before gathering for a friendly conversation.\nUltimately, blending modern convenience with timeless values helps communities <u><b>enhance</b></u> their daily routines and help students develop self-study, teamwork and digital research skills in a sustainable way.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 8: New Ways to Learn)</i>"
