            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 8: NEW WAYS TO LEARN</b>\nPractising learning methods is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying blended learning, online courses and digital tools in education have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to combine face-to-face discussions with flexible online study.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to combine traditional and digital learning in modern classrooms this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps teachers and students help students develop self-study, teamwork and digital research skills with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 8: New Ways to Learn)</i>"
