            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 9: PROTECTING THE ENVIRONMENT</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying protecting biodiversity and stopping deforestation and wildlife trade have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When rangers and students see their close friends willingly engage in environmental programmes, they are far more likely to join in and prevent deforestation and protect endangered animals from poaching.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to protect natural habitats and biodiversity in national parks or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school maintain the natural balance of ecosystems and reduce air pollution.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 9: Protecting the Environment)</i>"
