            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 9: PROTECTING THE ENVIRONMENT</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about protecting biodiversity and stopping deforestation and wildlife trade. Rather than treating Unit 9: Protecting the Environment as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to prevent deforestation and protect endangered animals from poaching.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to protect natural habitats and biodiversity in national parks. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on environmental programmes. Through these authentic tasks, rangers and students discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped maintain the natural balance of ecosystems and reduce air pollution. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making protecting biodiversity and stopping deforestation and wildlife trade an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 9: Protecting the Environment)</i>"
