            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 9: PROTECTING THE ENVIRONMENT</b>\nPractising environmental programmes is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying protecting biodiversity and stopping deforestation and wildlife trade have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to prevent deforestation and protect endangered animals from poaching.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to protect natural habitats and biodiversity in national parks this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps rangers and students maintain the natural balance of ecosystems and reduce air pollution with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 9: Protecting the Environment)</i>"
