            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON ECOTOURISM</b>\nIn recent years, sustainable ecotourism, respecting local culture and protecting nature has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of eco-tourists and local guides recognise the importance of ecotourism tours, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>damaged</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in nature reserves. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to practise responsible ecotourism.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that educate travellers about local ecosystems and cultural traditions, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how ecotourism tours can provide sustainable income for local villagers to conserve nature.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should practise responsible ecotourism, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in nature reserves. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting sustainable ecotourism, respecting local culture and protecting nature matters for the long-term future. Young people who see eco-tourists and local guides model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, ecotourism tours not only educate travellers about local ecosystems and cultural traditions today but also provide sustainable income for local villagers to conserve nature for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 10: Ecotourism)</i>"
