            "passage": "<b>MAKING ECOTOURISM PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to practise responsible ecotourism may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed ecotourism tours help eco-tourists and local guides educate travellers about local ecosystems and cultural traditions. At the same time, these practices provide sustainable income for local villagers to conserve nature and foster stronger cooperation in nature reserves.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in ecotourism tours has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>sustainable</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. Eco-tourists and local guides can start with simple steps in nature reserves, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind sustainable ecotourism, respecting local culture and protecting nature become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>impact</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 10: Ecotourism)</i>"
