            "passage": "<b>COMMUNITY ACTION AND PEER MENTORING IN UNIT 10: ECOTOURISM</b>\nLast year, youth unions in three provinces launched a six-month pilot programme to promote sustainable ecotourism, respecting local culture and protecting nature at the grassroots level. Instead of relying on formal lectures, organisers trained two hundred high school volunteers to lead weekend activities where neighbours could practise responsible ecotourism in nature reserves. Within the first two months, local communities reported <u><b>tangible</b></u> improvements in daily habits and neighbourhood solidarity.\nA key factor behind this success was peer-to-peer mentoring. Teenagers often feel intimidated when adults lecture them, but they respond enthusiastically when classmates demonstrate how ecotourism tours can educate travellers about local ecosystems and cultural traditions. By working in small teams of five or six, participants shared practical tips, solved everyday problems together and helped one another provide sustainable income for local villagers to conserve nature.\nOrganisers also introduced simple tools to keep momentum high. <b>[I]</b> Every participating household received a visual progress board to track weekly milestones. <b>[II]</b> Local cultural centres hosted monthly gatherings where teams showcased their most creative ideas. <b>[III]</b> These celebrations turned routine tasks into a source of shared pride across the entire district. <b>[IV]</b>\nFollow-up surveys conducted six months later confirmed that over seventy-five per cent of participants continued practising ecotourism tours independently. When student volunteers take ownership of community initiatives, <u><b>their</b></u> enthusiasm inspires both younger children and older residents. The pilot programme demonstrates that youth-led action is one of the most powerful ways to bring the values of Unit 10: Ecotourism to life.\n<i>(Adapted from Community Development & Youth Studies – Unit 10: Ecotourism)</i>"
