            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 10: ECOTOURISM</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying sustainable ecotourism, respecting local culture and protecting nature have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When eco-tourists and local guides see their close friends willingly engage in ecotourism tours, they are far more likely to join in and educate travellers about local ecosystems and cultural traditions.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to practise responsible ecotourism in nature reserves or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school provide sustainable income for local villagers to conserve nature.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 10: Ecotourism)</i>"
