            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 10: ECOTOURISM</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that sustainable ecotourism, respecting local culture and protecting nature is important, yet still find it difficult to practise responsible ecotourism in nature reserves every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, eco-tourists and local guides can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on ecotourism tours. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners educate travellers about local ecosystems and cultural traditions without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts provide sustainable income for local villagers to conserve nature. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 10: Ecotourism report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, ecotourism tours stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 10: Ecotourism)</i>"
