            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 10: ECOTOURISM</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about sustainable ecotourism, respecting local culture and protecting nature. Rather than treating Unit 10: Ecotourism as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to educate travellers about local ecosystems and cultural traditions.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to practise responsible ecotourism in nature reserves. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on ecotourism tours. Through these authentic tasks, eco-tourists and local guides discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped provide sustainable income for local villagers to conserve nature. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making sustainable ecotourism, respecting local culture and protecting nature an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 10: Ecotourism)</i>"
