            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 10: ECOTOURISM</b>\nPractising ecotourism tours is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying sustainable ecotourism, respecting local culture and protecting nature have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to educate travellers about local ecosystems and cultural traditions.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to practise responsible ecotourism in nature reserves this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps eco-tourists and local guides provide sustainable income for local villagers to conserve nature with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 10: Ecotourism)</i>"
