            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON A LONG AND HEALTHY LIFE</b>\nIn recent years, maintaining a balanced diet, regular exercise and longevity has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of teenagers and parents recognise the importance of healthy habits, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>examined</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in daily life. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to maintain a balanced diet and regular workout.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that strengthen the immune system and prevent infectious diseases, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how healthy habits can increase life expectancy and boost daily mental energy.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should maintain a balanced diet and regular workout, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in daily life. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting maintaining a balanced diet, regular exercise and longevity matters for the long-term future. Young people who see teenagers and parents model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, healthy habits not only strengthen the immune system and prevent infectious diseases today but also increase life expectancy and boost daily mental energy for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 1: A Long and Healthy Life)</i>"
