            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 1: A LONG AND HEALTHY LIFE</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying maintaining a balanced diet, regular exercise and longevity have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When teenagers and parents see their close friends willingly engage in healthy habits, they are far more likely to join in and strengthen the immune system and prevent infectious diseases.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to maintain a balanced diet and regular workout in daily life or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school increase life expectancy and boost daily mental energy.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 1: A Long and Healthy Life)</i>"
