            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 1: A LONG AND HEALTHY LIFE</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that maintaining a balanced diet, regular exercise and longevity is important, yet still find it difficult to maintain a balanced diet and regular workout in daily life every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, teenagers and parents can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on healthy habits. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners strengthen the immune system and prevent infectious diseases without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts increase life expectancy and boost daily mental energy. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 1: A Long and Healthy Life report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, healthy habits stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 1: A Long and Healthy Life)</i>"
