            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 2: THE GENERATION GAP</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying understanding generational differences and bridging the generation gap have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When parents and teenagers see their close friends willingly engage in generational differences, they are far more likely to join in and reduce family conflicts over screen time, hairstyles and career choices.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to respect different generational viewpoints in extended and nuclear families or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school build mutual respect between grandparents, parents and Gen Z children.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 2: The Generation Gap)</i>"
