            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 2: THE GENERATION GAP</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that understanding generational differences and bridging the generation gap is important, yet still find it difficult to respect different generational viewpoints in extended and nuclear families every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, parents and teenagers can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on generational differences. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners reduce family conflicts over screen time, hairstyles and career choices without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts build mutual respect between grandparents, parents and Gen Z children. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 2: The Generation Gap report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, generational differences stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 2: The Generation Gap)</i>"
