            "passage": "<b>COMBINING TRADITIONAL VALUES WITH MODERN TOOLS IN UNIT 2: THE GENERATION GAP</b>\nIn the digital age, some commentators worry that modern technology might distract young people from understanding generational differences and bridging the generation gap. Yet recent educational projects show that when used wisely, digital tools can actually strengthen generational differences. Shared mobile calendars, reminder apps and online group checklists make it easier than ever for parents and teenagers to reduce family conflicts over screen time, hairstyles and career choices.\nOf course, technology alone cannot solve every problem. Researchers warn against relying passively on apps while ignoring face-to-face communication, as <u><b>they</b></u> are only tools in the hands of users. <u><b>Digital tools should serve as a practical bridge to support daily cooperation rather than a replacement for genuine human interaction.</b></u>\nWhen families and classmates combine smart reminders with warm personal encouragement, they can <u><b>preserve</b></u> the core spirit of Unit 2: The Generation Gap. For example, a quick evening message can remind everyone to respect different generational viewpoints in extended and nuclear families before gathering for a friendly conversation.\nUltimately, blending modern convenience with timeless values helps communities <u><b>enhance</b></u> their daily routines and build mutual respect between grandparents, parents and Gen Z children in a sustainable way.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 2: The Generation Gap)</i>"
