            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 2: THE GENERATION GAP</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about understanding generational differences and bridging the generation gap. Rather than treating Unit 2: The Generation Gap as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to reduce family conflicts over screen time, hairstyles and career choices.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to respect different generational viewpoints in extended and nuclear families. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on generational differences. Through these authentic tasks, parents and teenagers discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped build mutual respect between grandparents, parents and Gen Z children. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making understanding generational differences and bridging the generation gap an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 2: The Generation Gap)</i>"
