            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 2: THE GENERATION GAP</b>\nPractising generational differences is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying understanding generational differences and bridging the generation gap have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to reduce family conflicts over screen time, hairstyles and career choices.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to respect different generational viewpoints in extended and nuclear families this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps parents and teenagers build mutual respect between grandparents, parents and Gen Z children with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 2: The Generation Gap)</i>"
