            "passage": "<b>MAKING CITIES OF THE FUTURE PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to build sustainable and liveable smart cities may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed smart technologies help city dwellers and planners use AI sensors to save energy and eliminate traffic jams. At the same time, these practices expand rooftop gardens and pedestrian zones for a greener environment and foster stronger cooperation in future urban centres.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in smart technologies has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>liveable</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. City dwellers and planners can start with simple steps in future urban centres, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind smart cities, green skyscrapers and sustainable urban living become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>dweller</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 3: Cities of the Future)</i>"
