            "passage": "<b>WHY CONSISTENCY MATTERS MORE THAN PERFECTION IN UNIT 3: CITIES OF THE FUTURE</b>\nWhen people first decide to focus on smart cities, green skyscrapers and sustainable urban living, they often set overly ambitious targets. Enthusiastic beginners might promise to transform their entire lifestyle in a single weekend, only to feel exhausted a few days later. Studies on habit formation show that well-paced smart technologies are far more effective when built step by step, helping city dwellers and planners use AI sensors to save energy and eliminate traffic jams without burnout.\nThe main reason crash campaigns fail is that <u><b>they</b></u> demand too much willpower at once. <u><b>Small, consistent efforts practised every day produce far more lasting results than occasional bursts of intense activity.</b></u> When a routine fits naturally into a family’s or student’s daily schedule, it requires less mental strain to maintain.\nExperts therefore recommend a <u><b>gradual</b></u> approach to build sustainable and liveable smart cities in future urban centres. By starting with one simple task each day and celebrating weekly milestones, learners steadily build momentum. Such steady practice not only strengthens personal discipline but also helps communities expand rooftop gardens and pedestrian zones for a greener environment.\nOf course, occasional setbacks are normal during busy exam weeks or family events. Rather than abandoning the goal altogether, resilient learners simply resume their routine the next morning, knowing that patience will <u><b>foster</b></u> long-term success.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 3: Cities of the Future)</i>"
