            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 3: CITIES OF THE FUTURE</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about smart cities, green skyscrapers and sustainable urban living. Rather than treating Unit 3: Cities of the Future as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to use AI sensors to save energy and eliminate traffic jams.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to build sustainable and liveable smart cities in future urban centres. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on smart technologies. Through these authentic tasks, city dwellers and planners discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped expand rooftop gardens and pedestrian zones for a greener environment. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making smart cities, green skyscrapers and sustainable urban living an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 3: Cities of the Future)</i>"
