            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 3: CITIES OF THE FUTURE</b>\nPractising smart technologies is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying smart cities, green skyscrapers and sustainable urban living have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to use AI sensors to save energy and eliminate traffic jams.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to build sustainable and liveable smart cities in future urban centres this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps city dwellers and planners expand rooftop gardens and pedestrian zones for a greener environment with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 3: Cities of the Future)</i>"
