            "passage": "<b>MAKING ASEAN AND VIET NAM PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to participate actively in ASEAN youth projects may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed cultural exchange programmes help young delegates from ASEAN nations promote solidarity, mutual understanding and peace among member states. At the same time, these practices develop leadership, communication and teamwork skills for youth and foster stronger cooperation across the ASEAN region.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in cultural exchange programmes has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>qualified</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. Young delegates from asean nations can start with simple steps across the ASEAN region, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind ASEAN youth cooperation, cultural exchange and leadership become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>contribution</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 4: ASEAN and Viet Nam)</i>"
