            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 4: ASEAN AND VIET NAM</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying ASEAN youth cooperation, cultural exchange and leadership have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When young delegates from ASEAN nations see their close friends willingly engage in cultural exchange programmes, they are far more likely to join in and promote solidarity, mutual understanding and peace among member states.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to participate actively in ASEAN youth projects across the ASEAN region or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school develop leadership, communication and teamwork skills for youth.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 4: ASEAN and Viet Nam)</i>"
