            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 4: ASEAN AND VIET NAM</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that ASEAN youth cooperation, cultural exchange and leadership is important, yet still find it difficult to participate actively in ASEAN youth projects across the ASEAN region every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, young delegates from ASEAN nations can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on cultural exchange programmes. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners promote solidarity, mutual understanding and peace among member states without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts develop leadership, communication and teamwork skills for youth. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 4: ASEAN and Viet Nam report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, cultural exchange programmes stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 4: ASEAN and Viet Nam)</i>"
