            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 4: ASEAN AND VIET NAM</b>\nPractising cultural exchange programmes is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying ASEAN youth cooperation, cultural exchange and leadership have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to promote solidarity, mutual understanding and peace among member states.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to participate actively in ASEAN youth projects across the ASEAN region this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps young delegates from ASEAN nations develop leadership, communication and teamwork skills for youth with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 4: ASEAN and Viet Nam)</i>"
