            "explanation": "Trong ngữ cảnh đoạn 1, 'trapped' đồng nghĩa với 'held'.",
            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON GLOBAL WARMING</b>\nIn recent years, cutting greenhouse gas emissions, soot and deforestation has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of governments and citizens recognise the importance of climate actions, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>trapped</b></u> these practices into their daily routines around the world. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to switch from fossil fuels to renewable energy.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that cut down on carbon dioxide, methane and black carbon emissions, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how climate actions can slow down polar ice melting and protect coastal communities.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should switch from fossil fuels to renewable energy, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role around the world. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting cutting greenhouse gas emissions, soot and deforestation matters for the long-term future. Young people who see governments and citizens model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, climate actions not only cut down on carbon dioxide, methane and black carbon emissions today but also slow down polar ice melting and protect coastal communities for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 5: Global Warming)</i>"
