            "passage": "<b>WHY CONSISTENCY MATTERS MORE THAN PERFECTION IN UNIT 5: GLOBAL WARMING</b>\nWhen people first decide to focus on cutting greenhouse gas emissions, soot and deforestation, they often set overly ambitious targets. Enthusiastic beginners might promise to transform their entire lifestyle in a single weekend, only to feel exhausted a few days later. Studies on habit formation show that well-paced climate actions are far more effective when built step by step, helping governments and citizens cut down on carbon dioxide, methane and black carbon emissions without burnout.\nThe main reason crash campaigns fail is that <u><b>they</b></u> demand too much willpower at once. <u><b>Small, consistent efforts practised every day produce far more lasting results than occasional bursts of intense activity.</b></u> When a routine fits naturally into a family’s or student’s daily schedule, it requires less mental strain to maintain.\nExperts therefore recommend a <u><b>gradual</b></u> approach to switch from fossil fuels to renewable energy around the world. By starting with one simple task each day and celebrating weekly milestones, learners steadily build momentum. Such steady practice not only strengthens personal discipline but also helps communities slow down polar ice melting and protect coastal communities.\nOf course, occasional setbacks are normal during busy exam weeks or family events. Rather than abandoning the goal altogether, resilient learners simply resume their routine the next morning, knowing that patience will <u><b>foster</b></u> long-term success.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 5: Global Warming)</i>"
