            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 5: GLOBAL WARMING</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying cutting greenhouse gas emissions, soot and deforestation have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When governments and citizens see their close friends willingly engage in climate actions, they are far more likely to join in and cut down on carbon dioxide, methane and black carbon emissions.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to switch from fossil fuels to renewable energy around the world or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school slow down polar ice melting and protect coastal communities.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 5: Global Warming)</i>"
