            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 5: GLOBAL WARMING</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that cutting greenhouse gas emissions, soot and deforestation is important, yet still find it difficult to switch from fossil fuels to renewable energy around the world every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, governments and citizens can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on climate actions. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners cut down on carbon dioxide, methane and black carbon emissions without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts slow down polar ice melting and protect coastal communities. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 5: Global Warming report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, climate actions stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 5: Global Warming)</i>"
