            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 5: GLOBAL WARMING</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about cutting greenhouse gas emissions, soot and deforestation. Rather than treating Unit 5: Global Warming as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to cut down on carbon dioxide, methane and black carbon emissions.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to switch from fossil fuels to renewable energy around the world. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on climate actions. Through these authentic tasks, governments and citizens discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped slow down polar ice melting and protect coastal communities. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making cutting greenhouse gas emissions, soot and deforestation an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 5: Global Warming)</i>"
