            "passage": "<b>MAKING PRESERVING OUR HERITAGE PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to preserve and promote our national heritage may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed heritage sites help young people and local artisans pass down traditional folk arts like Quan Họ and Đờn Ca Tài Tử. At the same time, these practices protect ancient monuments and scenic landscapes from damage and foster stronger cooperation across Viet Nam.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in heritage sites has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>ancient</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. Young people and local artisans can start with simple steps across Viet Nam, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind preserving cultural and natural heritage sites across Viet Nam become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>monument</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 6: Preserving Our Heritage)</i>"
