            "passage": "<b>WHY CONSISTENCY MATTERS MORE THAN PERFECTION IN UNIT 6: PRESERVING OUR HERITAGE</b>\nWhen people first decide to focus on preserving cultural and natural heritage sites across Viet Nam, they often set overly ambitious targets. Enthusiastic beginners might promise to transform their entire lifestyle in a single weekend, only to feel exhausted a few days later. Studies on habit formation show that well-paced heritage sites are far more effective when built step by step, helping young people and local artisans pass down traditional folk arts like Quan Họ and Đờn Ca Tài Tử without burnout.\nThe main reason crash campaigns fail is that <u><b>they</b></u> demand too much willpower at once. <u><b>Small, consistent efforts practised every day produce far more lasting results than occasional bursts of intense activity.</b></u> When a routine fits naturally into a family’s or student’s daily schedule, it requires less mental strain to maintain.\nExperts therefore recommend a <u><b>gradual</b></u> approach to preserve and promote our national heritage across Viet Nam. By starting with one simple task each day and celebrating weekly milestones, learners steadily build momentum. Such steady practice not only strengthens personal discipline but also helps communities protect ancient monuments and scenic landscapes from damage.\nOf course, occasional setbacks are normal during busy exam weeks or family events. Rather than abandoning the goal altogether, resilient learners simply resume their routine the next morning, knowing that patience will <u><b>foster</b></u> long-term success.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 6: Preserving Our Heritage)</i>"
