            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 6: PRESERVING OUR HERITAGE</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying preserving cultural and natural heritage sites across Viet Nam have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When young people and local artisans see their close friends willingly engage in heritage sites, they are far more likely to join in and pass down traditional folk arts like Quan Họ and Đờn Ca Tài Tử.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to preserve and promote our national heritage across Viet Nam or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school protect ancient monuments and scenic landscapes from damage.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 6: Preserving Our Heritage)</i>"
