            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 6: PRESERVING OUR HERITAGE</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that preserving cultural and natural heritage sites across Viet Nam is important, yet still find it difficult to preserve and promote our national heritage across Viet Nam every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, young people and local artisans can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on heritage sites. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners pass down traditional folk arts like Quan Họ and Đờn Ca Tài Tử without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts protect ancient monuments and scenic landscapes from damage. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 6: Preserving Our Heritage report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, heritage sites stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 6: Preserving Our Heritage)</i>"
