            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 6: PRESERVING OUR HERITAGE</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about preserving cultural and natural heritage sites across Viet Nam. Rather than treating Unit 6: Preserving Our Heritage as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to pass down traditional folk arts like Quan Họ and Đờn Ca Tài Tử.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to preserve and promote our national heritage across Viet Nam. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on heritage sites. Through these authentic tasks, young people and local artisans discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped protect ancient monuments and scenic landscapes from damage. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making preserving cultural and natural heritage sites across Viet Nam an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 6: Preserving Our Heritage)</i>"
