            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 6: PRESERVING OUR HERITAGE</b>\nPractising heritage sites is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying preserving cultural and natural heritage sites across Viet Nam have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to pass down traditional folk arts like Quan Họ and Đờn Ca Tài Tử.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to preserve and promote our national heritage across Viet Nam this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps young people and local artisans protect ancient monuments and scenic landscapes from damage with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 6: Preserving Our Heritage)</i>"
