            "explanation": "reluctant = ngần ngại, miễn cưỡng (hesitant and unwilling).",
            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 7: EDUCATION OPTIONS FOR SCHOOL-LEAVERS</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that choosing between university study and vocational training after high school is important, yet still find it difficult to choose the right educational pathway after graduation at universities and vocational colleges every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, school-leavers and parents can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on education options. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners gain practical hands-on skills through apprenticeships without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts earn formal degrees or trade qualifications that match career goals. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 7: Education Options for School-Leavers report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, education options stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 7: Education Options for School-Leavers)</i>"
