            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 7: EDUCATION OPTIONS FOR SCHOOL-LEAVERS</b>\nPractising education options is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying choosing between university study and vocational training after high school have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to gain practical hands-on skills through apprenticeships.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to choose the right educational pathway after graduation at universities and vocational colleges this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps school-leavers and parents earn formal degrees or trade qualifications that match career goals with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 7: Education Options for School-Leavers)</i>"
