            "explanation": "Trong ngữ cảnh đoạn 1, 'managed' đồng nghĩa với 'handled'.",
            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON BECOMING INDEPENDENT</b>\nIn recent years, developing time-management, budgeting and self-study skills for independent living has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of teenagers and university freshmen recognise the importance of independent life skills, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>managed</b></u> these practices into their daily routines at school and in daily life. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to develop essential life skills for independence.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that manage time, pocket money and household chores without reminders, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how independent life skills can build self-motivation, confidence and the trust of parents.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should develop essential life skills for independence, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role at school and in daily life. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting developing time-management, budgeting and self-study skills for independent living matters for the long-term future. Young people who see teenagers and university freshmen model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, independent life skills not only manage time, pocket money and household chores without reminders today but also build self-motivation, confidence and the trust of parents for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 8: Becoming Independent)</i>"
