            "passage": "<b>WHY CONSISTENCY MATTERS MORE THAN PERFECTION IN UNIT 8: BECOMING INDEPENDENT</b>\nWhen people first decide to focus on developing time-management, budgeting and self-study skills for independent living, they often set overly ambitious targets. Enthusiastic beginners might promise to transform their entire lifestyle in a single weekend, only to feel exhausted a few days later. Studies on habit formation show that well-paced independent life skills are far more effective when built step by step, helping teenagers and university freshmen manage time, pocket money and household chores without reminders without burnout.\nThe main reason crash campaigns fail is that <u><b>they</b></u> demand too much willpower at once. <u><b>Small, consistent efforts practised every day produce far more lasting results than occasional bursts of intense activity.</b></u> When a routine fits naturally into a family’s or student’s daily schedule, it requires less mental strain to maintain.\nExperts therefore recommend a <u><b>gradual</b></u> approach to develop essential life skills for independence at school and in daily life. By starting with one simple task each day and celebrating weekly milestones, learners steadily build momentum. Such steady practice not only strengthens personal discipline but also helps communities build self-motivation, confidence and the trust of parents.\nOf course, occasional setbacks are normal during busy exam weeks or family events. Rather than abandoning the goal altogether, resilient learners simply resume their routine the next morning, knowing that patience will <u><b>foster</b></u> long-term success.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 8: Becoming Independent)</i>"
