            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 8: BECOMING INDEPENDENT</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying developing time-management, budgeting and self-study skills for independent living have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When teenagers and university freshmen see their close friends willingly engage in independent life skills, they are far more likely to join in and manage time, pocket money and household chores without reminders.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to develop essential life skills for independence at school and in daily life or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school build self-motivation, confidence and the trust of parents.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 8: Becoming Independent)</i>"
