            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 8: BECOMING INDEPENDENT</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that developing time-management, budgeting and self-study skills for independent living is important, yet still find it difficult to develop essential life skills for independence at school and in daily life every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, teenagers and university freshmen can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on independent life skills. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners manage time, pocket money and household chores without reminders without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts build self-motivation, confidence and the trust of parents. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 8: Becoming Independent report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, independent life skills stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 8: Becoming Independent)</i>"
