            "passage": "<b>COMBINING TRADITIONAL VALUES WITH MODERN TOOLS IN UNIT 8: BECOMING INDEPENDENT</b>\nIn the digital age, some commentators worry that modern technology might distract young people from developing time-management, budgeting and self-study skills for independent living. Yet recent educational projects show that when used wisely, digital tools can actually strengthen independent life skills. Shared mobile calendars, reminder apps and online group checklists make it easier than ever for teenagers and university freshmen to manage time, pocket money and household chores without reminders.\nOf course, technology alone cannot solve every problem. Researchers warn against relying passively on apps while ignoring face-to-face communication, as <u><b>they</b></u> are only tools in the hands of users. <u><b>Digital tools should serve as a practical bridge to support daily cooperation rather than a replacement for genuine human interaction.</b></u>\nWhen families and classmates combine smart reminders with warm personal encouragement, they can <u><b>preserve</b></u> the core spirit of Unit 8: Becoming Independent. For example, a quick evening message can remind everyone to develop essential life skills for independence at school and in daily life before gathering for a friendly conversation.\nUltimately, blending modern convenience with timeless values helps communities <u><b>enhance</b></u> their daily routines and build self-motivation, confidence and the trust of parents in a sustainable way.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 8: Becoming Independent)</i>"
