            "explanation": "indispensable = vô cùng cần thiết, không thể thiếu (essential and vital).",
            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 8: BECOMING INDEPENDENT</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about developing time-management, budgeting and self-study skills for independent living. Rather than treating Unit 8: Becoming Independent as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to manage time, pocket money and household chores without reminders.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to develop essential life skills for independence at school and in daily life. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on independent life skills. Through these authentic tasks, teenagers and university freshmen discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped build self-motivation, confidence and the trust of parents. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making developing time-management, budgeting and self-study skills for independent living an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 8: Becoming Independent)</i>"
