            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 8: BECOMING INDEPENDENT</b>\nPractising independent life skills is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying developing time-management, budgeting and self-study skills for independent living have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to manage time, pocket money and household chores without reminders.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to develop essential life skills for independence at school and in daily life this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps teenagers and university freshmen build self-motivation, confidence and the trust of parents with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 8: Becoming Independent)</i>"
