            "passage": "<b>MAKING SOCIAL ISSUES PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to stand up against bullying and support victims may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed social issues help students, counsellors and parents protect teenagers from cyberbullying, body shaming and negative peer pressure. At the same time, these practices create a safe, empathetic and supportive school environment for all and foster stronger cooperation in schools and online.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in social issues has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>supportive</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. Students, counsellors and parents can start with simple steps in schools and online, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind addressing peer pressure, cyberbullying and body shaming among teens become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>campaign</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 9: Social Issues)</i>"
