            "passage": "<b>COMMUNITY ACTION AND PEER MENTORING IN UNIT 9: SOCIAL ISSUES</b>\nLast year, youth unions in three provinces launched a six-month pilot programme to promote addressing peer pressure, cyberbullying and body shaming among teens at the grassroots level. Instead of relying on formal lectures, organisers trained two hundred high school volunteers to lead weekend activities where neighbours could stand up against bullying and support victims in schools and online. Within the first two months, local communities reported <u><b>tangible</b></u> improvements in daily habits and neighbourhood solidarity.\nA key factor behind this success was peer-to-peer mentoring. Teenagers often feel intimidated when adults lecture them, but they respond enthusiastically when classmates demonstrate how social issues can protect teenagers from cyberbullying, body shaming and negative peer pressure. By working in small teams of five or six, participants shared practical tips, solved everyday problems together and helped one another create a safe, empathetic and supportive school environment for all.\nOrganisers also introduced simple tools to keep momentum high. <b>[I]</b> Every participating household received a visual progress board to track weekly milestones. <b>[II]</b> Local cultural centres hosted monthly gatherings where teams showcased their most creative ideas. <b>[III]</b> These celebrations turned routine tasks into a source of shared pride across the entire district. <b>[IV]</b>\nFollow-up surveys conducted six months later confirmed that over seventy-five per cent of participants continued practising social issues independently. When student volunteers take ownership of community initiatives, <u><b>their</b></u> enthusiasm inspires both younger children and older residents. The pilot programme demonstrates that youth-led action is one of the most powerful ways to bring the values of Unit 9: Social Issues to life.\n<i>(Adapted from Community Development & Youth Studies – Unit 9: Social Issues)</i>"
