            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 9: SOCIAL ISSUES</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying addressing peer pressure, cyberbullying and body shaming among teens have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When students, counsellors and parents see their close friends willingly engage in social issues, they are far more likely to join in and protect teenagers from cyberbullying, body shaming and negative peer pressure.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to stand up against bullying and support victims in schools and online or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school create a safe, empathetic and supportive school environment for all.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 9: Social Issues)</i>"
