            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 9: SOCIAL ISSUES</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that addressing peer pressure, cyberbullying and body shaming among teens is important, yet still find it difficult to stand up against bullying and support victims in schools and online every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, students, counsellors and parents can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on social issues. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners protect teenagers from cyberbullying, body shaming and negative peer pressure without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts create a safe, empathetic and supportive school environment for all. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 9: Social Issues report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, social issues stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 9: Social Issues)</i>"
