            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 9: SOCIAL ISSUES</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about addressing peer pressure, cyberbullying and body shaming among teens. Rather than treating Unit 9: Social Issues as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to protect teenagers from cyberbullying, body shaming and negative peer pressure.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to stand up against bullying and support victims in schools and online. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on social issues. Through these authentic tasks, students, counsellors and parents discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped create a safe, empathetic and supportive school environment for all. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making addressing peer pressure, cyberbullying and body shaming among teens an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 9: Social Issues)</i>"
