            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON THE ECOSYSTEM</b>\nIn recent years, conserving native flora and fauna, wetlands and food chains has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of biologists, rangers and students recognise the importance of natural ecosystems, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>restored</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in national parks and wetlands. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to conserve biodiversity and restore degraded habitats.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that protect native flora and fauna and keep food chains in balance, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how natural ecosystems can prevent soil erosion, store carbon and shelter migratory birds.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should conserve biodiversity and restore degraded habitats, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in national parks and wetlands. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting conserving native flora and fauna, wetlands and food chains matters for the long-term future. Young people who see biologists, rangers and students model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, natural ecosystems not only protect native flora and fauna and keep food chains in balance today but also prevent soil erosion, store carbon and shelter migratory birds for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 10: The Ecosystem)</i>"
