            "answer": 1,
            "explanation": "Đoạn 1 trình bày các kết quả nghiên cứu làm nổi bật lợi ích của natural ecosystems.",
            "passage": "<b>MAKING THE ECOSYSTEM PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to conserve biodiversity and restore degraded habitats may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed natural ecosystems help biologists, rangers and students protect native flora and fauna and keep food chains in balance. At the same time, these practices prevent soil erosion, store carbon and shelter migratory birds and foster stronger cooperation in national parks and wetlands.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in natural ecosystems has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>native</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. Biologists, rangers and students can start with simple steps in national parks and wetlands, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind conserving native flora and fauna, wetlands and food chains become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>species</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 10: The Ecosystem)</i>"
