            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 10: THE ECOSYSTEM</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying conserving native flora and fauna, wetlands and food chains have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When biologists, rangers and students see their close friends willingly engage in natural ecosystems, they are far more likely to join in and protect native flora and fauna and keep food chains in balance.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to conserve biodiversity and restore degraded habitats in national parks and wetlands or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school prevent soil erosion, store carbon and shelter migratory birds.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 10: The Ecosystem)</i>"
