            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 10: THE ECOSYSTEM</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that conserving native flora and fauna, wetlands and food chains is important, yet still find it difficult to conserve biodiversity and restore degraded habitats in national parks and wetlands every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, biologists, rangers and students can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on natural ecosystems. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners protect native flora and fauna and keep food chains in balance without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts prevent soil erosion, store carbon and shelter migratory birds. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 10: The Ecosystem report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, natural ecosystems stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 10: The Ecosystem)</i>"
