            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 10: THE ECOSYSTEM</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about conserving native flora and fauna, wetlands and food chains. Rather than treating Unit 10: The Ecosystem as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to protect native flora and fauna and keep food chains in balance.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to conserve biodiversity and restore degraded habitats in national parks and wetlands. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on natural ecosystems. Through these authentic tasks, biologists, rangers and students discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped prevent soil erosion, store carbon and shelter migratory birds. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making conserving native flora and fauna, wetlands and food chains an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 10: The Ecosystem)</i>"
