            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 10: THE ECOSYSTEM</b>\nPractising natural ecosystems is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying conserving native flora and fauna, wetlands and food chains have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to protect native flora and fauna and keep food chains in balance.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to conserve biodiversity and restore degraded habitats in national parks and wetlands this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps biologists, rangers and students prevent soil erosion, store carbon and shelter migratory birds with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 10: The Ecosystem)</i>"
