            "passage": "<b>MAKING LIFE STORIES WE ADMIRE PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to admire and learn from great life stories may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed inspiring biographies help young readers and historians learn about the determination and selflessness of national heroes. At the same time, these practices inspire teenagers to overcome obstacles and contribute to society and foster stronger cooperation in history and modern life.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in inspiring biographies has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>ambitious</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. Young readers and historians can start with simple steps in history and modern life, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind admiring inspiring life stories, national heroes and innovators become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>achievement</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 1: Life Stories We Admire)</i>"
