            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 1: LIFE STORIES WE ADMIRE</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying admiring inspiring life stories, national heroes and innovators have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When young readers and historians see their close friends willingly engage in inspiring biographies, they are far more likely to join in and learn about the determination and selflessness of national heroes.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to admire and learn from great life stories in history and modern life or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school inspire teenagers to overcome obstacles and contribute to society.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 1: Life Stories We Admire)</i>"
