            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 1: LIFE STORIES WE ADMIRE</b>\nPractising inspiring biographies is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying admiring inspiring life stories, national heroes and innovators have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to learn about the determination and selflessness of national heroes.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to admire and learn from great life stories in history and modern life this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps young readers and historians inspire teenagers to overcome obstacles and contribute to society with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 1: Life Stories We Admire)</i>"
