            "passage": "<b>COMMUNITY ACTION AND PEER MENTORING IN UNIT 2: A MULTICULTURAL WORLD</b>\nLast year, youth unions in three provinces launched a six-month pilot programme to promote celebrating cultural diversity, traditional festivals and global cuisines at the grassroots level. Instead of relying on formal lectures, organisers trained two hundred high school volunteers to lead weekend activities where neighbours could respect and celebrate cultural diversity in a multicultural world. Within the first two months, local communities reported <u><b>tangible</b></u> improvements in daily habits and neighbourhood solidarity.\nA key factor behind this success was peer-to-peer mentoring. Teenagers often feel intimidated when adults lecture them, but they respond enthusiastically when classmates demonstrate how cultural traditions can help people overcome culture shock and appreciate cultural diversity. By working in small teams of five or six, participants shared practical tips, solved everyday problems together and helped one another preserve national cultural identity in an age of globalisation.\nOrganisers also introduced simple tools to keep momentum high. <b>[I]</b> Every participating household received a visual progress board to track weekly milestones. <b>[II]</b> Local cultural centres hosted monthly gatherings where teams showcased their most creative ideas. <b>[III]</b> These celebrations turned routine tasks into a source of shared pride across the entire district. <b>[IV]</b>\nFollow-up surveys conducted six months later confirmed that over seventy-five per cent of participants continued practising cultural traditions independently. When student volunteers take ownership of community initiatives, <u><b>their</b></u> enthusiasm inspires both younger children and older residents. The pilot programme demonstrates that youth-led action is one of the most powerful ways to bring the values of Unit 2: A Multicultural World to life.\n<i>(Adapted from Community Development & Youth Studies – Unit 2: A Multicultural World)</i>"
