            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 2: A MULTICULTURAL WORLD</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying celebrating cultural diversity, traditional festivals and global cuisines have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When students from diverse backgrounds see their close friends willingly engage in cultural traditions, they are far more likely to join in and help people overcome culture shock and appreciate cultural diversity.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to respect and celebrate cultural diversity in a multicultural world or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school preserve national cultural identity in an age of globalisation.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 2: A Multicultural World)</i>"
