            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 2: A MULTICULTURAL WORLD</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that celebrating cultural diversity, traditional festivals and global cuisines is important, yet still find it difficult to respect and celebrate cultural diversity in a multicultural world every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, students from diverse backgrounds can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on cultural traditions. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners help people overcome culture shock and appreciate cultural diversity without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts preserve national cultural identity in an age of globalisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 2: A Multicultural World report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, cultural traditions stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 2: A Multicultural World)</i>"
