            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 2: A MULTICULTURAL WORLD</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about celebrating cultural diversity, traditional festivals and global cuisines. Rather than treating Unit 2: A Multicultural World as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to help people overcome culture shock and appreciate cultural diversity.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to respect and celebrate cultural diversity in a multicultural world. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on cultural traditions. Through these authentic tasks, students from diverse backgrounds discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped preserve national cultural identity in an age of globalisation. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making celebrating cultural diversity, traditional festivals and global cuisines an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 2: A Multicultural World)</i>"
