            "passage": "<b>MAKING GREEN LIVING PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to practise green living and waste recycling may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed green-living habits help students and households reduce plastic packaging sent to landfills and save water and electricity. At the same time, these practices turn fruit peels and leftovers into nutrient-rich compost for gardens and foster stronger cooperation at school and at home.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in green-living habits has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>reusable</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. Students and households can start with simple steps at school and at home, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind reducing plastic waste, composting leftovers and adopting green living become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>container</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 3: Green Living)</i>"
