            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 3: GREEN LIVING</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that reducing plastic waste, composting leftovers and adopting green living is important, yet still find it difficult to practise green living and waste recycling at school and at home every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, students and households can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on green-living habits. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners reduce plastic packaging sent to landfills and save water and electricity without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts turn fruit peels and leftovers into nutrient-rich compost for gardens. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 3: Green Living report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, green-living habits stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 3: Green Living)</i>"
