            "passage": "<b>COMBINING TRADITIONAL VALUES WITH MODERN TOOLS IN UNIT 3: GREEN LIVING</b>\nIn the digital age, some commentators worry that modern technology might distract young people from reducing plastic waste, composting leftovers and adopting green living. Yet recent educational projects show that when used wisely, digital tools can actually strengthen green-living habits. Shared mobile calendars, reminder apps and online group checklists make it easier than ever for students and households to reduce plastic packaging sent to landfills and save water and electricity.\nOf course, technology alone cannot solve every problem. Researchers warn against relying passively on apps while ignoring face-to-face communication, as <u><b>they</b></u> are only tools in the hands of users. <u><b>Digital tools should serve as a practical bridge to support daily cooperation rather than a replacement for genuine human interaction.</b></u>\nWhen families and classmates combine smart reminders with warm personal encouragement, they can <u><b>preserve</b></u> the core spirit of Unit 3: Green Living. For example, a quick evening message can remind everyone to practise green living and waste recycling at school and at home before gathering for a friendly conversation.\nUltimately, blending modern convenience with timeless values helps communities <u><b>enhance</b></u> their daily routines and turn fruit peels and leftovers into nutrient-rich compost for gardens in a sustainable way.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 3: Green Living)</i>"
