            "explanation": "indispensable = vô cùng cần thiết, không thể thiếu (essential and vital).",
            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 3: GREEN LIVING</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about reducing plastic waste, composting leftovers and adopting green living. Rather than treating Unit 3: Green Living as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to reduce plastic packaging sent to landfills and save water and electricity.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to practise green living and waste recycling at school and at home. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on green-living habits. Through these authentic tasks, students and households discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped turn fruit peels and leftovers into nutrient-rich compost for gardens. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making reducing plastic waste, composting leftovers and adopting green living an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 3: Green Living)</i>"
