            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 3: GREEN LIVING</b>\nPractising green-living habits is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying reducing plastic waste, composting leftovers and adopting green living have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to reduce plastic packaging sent to landfills and save water and electricity.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to practise green living and waste recycling at school and at home this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps students and households turn fruit peels and leftovers into nutrient-rich compost for gardens with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 3: Green Living)</i>"
