            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON URBANISATION</b>\nIn recent years, urbanisation trends, infrastructure challenges and sustainable city growth has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of city planners and residents recognise the importance of urban developments, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>expanded</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in rapidly growing cities. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to plan urban expansion sustainably.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that upgrade public transport, schools and hospitals for growing populations, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how urban developments can reduce traffic congestion, housing shortages and air pollution.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should plan urban expansion sustainably, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in rapidly growing cities. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting urbanisation trends, infrastructure challenges and sustainable city growth matters for the long-term future. Young people who see city planners and residents model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, urban developments not only upgrade public transport, schools and hospitals for growing populations today but also reduce traffic congestion, housing shortages and air pollution for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 4: Urbanisation)</i>"
