            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 4: URBANISATION</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying urbanisation trends, infrastructure challenges and sustainable city growth have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When city planners and residents see their close friends willingly engage in urban developments, they are far more likely to join in and upgrade public transport, schools and hospitals for growing populations.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to plan urban expansion sustainably in rapidly growing cities or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school reduce traffic congestion, housing shortages and air pollution.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 4: Urbanisation)</i>"
