            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 4: URBANISATION</b>\nPractising urban developments is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying urbanisation trends, infrastructure challenges and sustainable city growth have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to upgrade public transport, schools and hospitals for growing populations.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to plan urban expansion sustainably in rapidly growing cities this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps city planners and residents reduce traffic congestion, housing shortages and air pollution with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 4: Urbanisation)</i>"
