            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 5: THE WORLD OF WORK</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying job requirements, working conditions and career preparation have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When job applicants and employers see their close friends willingly engage in job opportunities, they are far more likely to join in and gain valuable on-the-job training and communication skills.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to prepare a strong CV and develop workplace skills in the modern workplace or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school earn a fair wage while learning time management and responsibility.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 5: The World of Work)</i>"
