            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 5: THE WORLD OF WORK</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about job requirements, working conditions and career preparation. Rather than treating Unit 5: The World of Work as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to gain valuable on-the-job training and communication skills.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to prepare a strong CV and develop workplace skills in the modern workplace. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on job opportunities. Through these authentic tasks, job applicants and employers discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped earn a fair wage while learning time management and responsibility. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making job requirements, working conditions and career preparation an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 5: The World of Work)</i>"
