            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 5: THE WORLD OF WORK</b>\nPractising job opportunities is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying job requirements, working conditions and career preparation have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to gain valuable on-the-job training and communication skills.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to prepare a strong CV and develop workplace skills in the modern workplace this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps job applicants and employers earn a fair wage while learning time management and responsibility with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 5: The World of Work)</i>"
