            "explanation": "Trong ngữ cảnh đoạn 1, 'programmed' đồng nghĩa với 'coded'.",
            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE</b>\nIn recent years, applications of artificial intelligence in education, healthcare and homes has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of students, teachers and engineers recognise the importance of AI applications, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>programmed</b></u> these practices into their daily routines in schools and smart homes. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to use artificial intelligence responsibly and effectively.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that provide personalised learning feedback and automate routine tasks, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how AI applications can help doctors analyse medical scans and assist elderly people at home.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should use artificial intelligence responsibly and effectively, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role in schools and smart homes. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting applications of artificial intelligence in education, healthcare and homes matters for the long-term future. Young people who see students, teachers and engineers model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, AI applications not only provide personalised learning feedback and automate routine tasks today but also help doctors analyse medical scans and assist elderly people at home for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 6: Artificial Intelligence)</i>"
