            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 6: ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying applications of artificial intelligence in education, healthcare and homes have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When students, teachers and engineers see their close friends willingly engage in AI applications, they are far more likely to join in and provide personalised learning feedback and automate routine tasks.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to use artificial intelligence responsibly and effectively in schools and smart homes or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school help doctors analyse medical scans and assist elderly people at home.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 6: Artificial Intelligence)</i>"
