            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 6: ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE</b>\nPractising AI applications is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying applications of artificial intelligence in education, healthcare and homes have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to provide personalised learning feedback and automate routine tasks.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to use artificial intelligence responsibly and effectively in schools and smart homes this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps students, teachers and engineers help doctors analyse medical scans and assist elderly people at home with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 6: Artificial Intelligence)</i>"
