            "explanation": "Trong ngữ cảnh đoạn 1, 'broadcast' đồng nghĩa với 'transmitted'.",
            "passage": "<b>PERSPECTIVES ON THE WORLD OF MASS MEDIA</b>\nIn recent years, digital media, fact-checking misinformation and responsible media use has attracted growing attention from educators and researchers alike. A recent national survey reports that while over eighty per cent of journalists and netizens recognise the importance of mass media channels, only about thirty-five per cent have <u><b>broadcast</b></u> these practices into their daily routines across digital and print media. Even when resources are available, many people still struggle to balance their busy schedules with the need to use mass media critically and responsibly.\nThe challenge lies not only in awareness but also in daily habits. Researchers note that active participants regularly engage in structured tasks that deliver breaking news quickly and connect communities worldwide, whereas passive observers only join occasional events once or twice a year. Consistent practice requires patience and planning, but it delivers lasting rewards. Many individuals simply repeat outdated habits they observed in the past, which prevents them from experiencing how mass media channels can help citizens verify credible sources and stop the spread of fake news.\nFortunately, attitudes are shifting rapidly. <b>[I]</b> Most respondents in the survey agreed that everyone should use mass media critically and responsibly, even though many admitted spending less than two hours a week on it themselves. <b>[II]</b> Progress was strongest in communities where schools and families worked together as partners. <b>[III]</b> Groups can begin by setting clear weekly goals and giving every member a practical role across digital and print media. <b>[IV]</b> Small, steady steps like these can turn a difficult challenge into a shared habit of cooperation.\nPromoting digital media, fact-checking misinformation and responsible media use matters for the long-term future. Young people who see journalists and netizens model positive behaviours are likely to view these practices as normal, and <u><b>they</b></u> will carry those values into adulthood. In this way, mass media channels not only deliver breaking news quickly and connect communities worldwide today but also help citizens verify credible sources and stop the spread of fake news for generations to come.\n<i>(Adapted from Educational & Social Research Reports – Unit 7: The World of Mass Media)</i>"
