            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 7: THE WORLD OF MASS MEDIA</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying digital media, fact-checking misinformation and responsible media use have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When journalists and netizens see their close friends willingly engage in mass media channels, they are far more likely to join in and deliver breaking news quickly and connect communities worldwide.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to use mass media critically and responsibly across digital and print media or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school help citizens verify credible sources and stop the spread of fake news.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 7: The World of Mass Media)</i>"
