            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 7: THE WORLD OF MASS MEDIA</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that digital media, fact-checking misinformation and responsible media use is important, yet still find it difficult to use mass media critically and responsibly across digital and print media every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, journalists and netizens can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on mass media channels. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners deliver breaking news quickly and connect communities worldwide without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts help citizens verify credible sources and stop the spread of fake news. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 7: The World of Mass Media report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, mass media channels stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 7: The World of Mass Media)</i>"
