            "explanation": "Đoạn 1 trình bày các kết quả nghiên cứu làm nổi bật lợi ích của conservation programmes.",
            "passage": "<b>MAKING WILDLIFE CONSERVATION PART OF DAILY LIFE</b>\nTaking time to conserve endangered wildlife and their natural habitats may seem ordinary at first glance, yet a growing body of research highlights its transformative power. Studies show that well-designed conservation programmes help conservationists and rangers rescue poached primates and sea turtles and release them back into the wild. At the same time, these practices protect forest habitats and enforce strict anti-poaching laws and foster stronger cooperation in nature reserves and rescue centres.\nDespite these clear advantages, participation in conservation programmes has faced obstacles in recent years. Researchers report that <u><b>they</b></u> have been neglected in many busy communities due to modern time pressures. <u><b>Tight timetables, heavy workloads and a lack of practical guidance can all prevent people from maintaining positive routines.</b></u> Even when individuals want to improve, distractions often get in the way.\nThe encouraging news is that success does not require perfection from day one. Experts emphasise that building <u><b>vulnerable</b></u> habits is about steady progress rather than instant results. Conservationists and rangers can start with simple steps in nature reserves and rescue centres, share responsibilities and use practical checklists to stay on track. Those who understand the purpose behind protecting endangered wildlife, rehabilitating primates and sea turtles become much more resilient and motivated.\nOf course, no single workshop or rule will solve every challenge overnight, and some groups need time to adapt. That is no reason to give up. Even one or two focused sessions a week can spark meaningful change, and the positive <u><b>enclosure</b></u> will grow stronger over time. What matters most is the commitment people bring to supporting one another every day.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 8: Wildlife Conservation)</i>"
