            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 8: WILDLIFE CONSERVATION</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying protecting endangered wildlife, rehabilitating primates and sea turtles have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When conservationists and rangers see their close friends willingly engage in conservation programmes, they are far more likely to join in and rescue poached primates and sea turtles and release them back into the wild.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to conserve endangered wildlife and their natural habitats in nature reserves and rescue centres or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school protect forest habitats and enforce strict anti-poaching laws.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 8: Wildlife Conservation)</i>"
