            "explanation": "reluctant = ngần ngại, miễn cưỡng (hesitant and unwilling).",
            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 8: WILDLIFE CONSERVATION</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that protecting endangered wildlife, rehabilitating primates and sea turtles is important, yet still find it difficult to conserve endangered wildlife and their natural habitats in nature reserves and rescue centres every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, conservationists and rangers can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on conservation programmes. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners rescue poached primates and sea turtles and release them back into the wild without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts protect forest habitats and enforce strict anti-poaching laws. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 8: Wildlife Conservation report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, conservation programmes stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 8: Wildlife Conservation)</i>"
