            "passage": "<b>COMBINING TRADITIONAL VALUES WITH MODERN TOOLS IN UNIT 8: WILDLIFE CONSERVATION</b>\nIn the digital age, some commentators worry that modern technology might distract young people from protecting endangered wildlife, rehabilitating primates and sea turtles. Yet recent educational projects show that when used wisely, digital tools can actually strengthen conservation programmes. Shared mobile calendars, reminder apps and online group checklists make it easier than ever for conservationists and rangers to rescue poached primates and sea turtles and release them back into the wild.\nOf course, technology alone cannot solve every problem. Researchers warn against relying passively on apps while ignoring face-to-face communication, as <u><b>they</b></u> are only tools in the hands of users. <u><b>Digital tools should serve as a practical bridge to support daily cooperation rather than a replacement for genuine human interaction.</b></u>\nWhen families and classmates combine smart reminders with warm personal encouragement, they can <u><b>preserve</b></u> the core spirit of Unit 8: Wildlife Conservation. For example, a quick evening message can remind everyone to conserve endangered wildlife and their natural habitats in nature reserves and rescue centres before gathering for a friendly conversation.\nUltimately, blending modern convenience with timeless values helps communities <u><b>enhance</b></u> their daily routines and protect forest habitats and enforce strict anti-poaching laws in a sustainable way.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 8: Wildlife Conservation)</i>"
