            "passage": "<b>PARTNERSHIPS BETWEEN SCHOOLS AND FAMILIES IN UNIT 9: CAREER PATHS</b>\nOver the past two years, a research team from the National Institute of Education has tracked 1,500 high school students to evaluate how exploring career paths, developing soft skills and adapting to automation affects teenager development. The study found that while nearly ninety per cent of school-leavers and career advisers valued career paths, only forty-two per cent <u><b>sustained</b></u> these routines throughout the entire school year. Students who tried to practise alone often gave up after a few weeks when exam pressure increased.\nBy contrast, learners whose families and teachers coordinated their guidance achieved far more durable results. In households where parents actively joined weekend activities to build both technical expertise and soft skills in the changing job market, teenagers were twice as likely to develop soft skills like critical thinking, teamwork and adaptability. Researchers explained that shared routines reduce the feeling of isolation and turn abstract classroom advice into enjoyable daily habits. Moreover, consistent family involvement helps keep up with technological changes and choose a fulfilling career without adding extra stress to students’ timetables.\nSchools across the country are now redesigning their programmes based on these findings. <b>[I]</b> Instead of assigning theoretical worksheets, teachers invite parents and students to co-sign a simple weekly action plan. <b>[II]</b> This joint commitment ensures that classroom lessons are reinforced at home every evening. <b>[III]</b> At monthly review sessions, classes celebrate collective progress and exchange practical solutions to common obstacles. <b>[IV]</b>\nThe long-term implications of this partnership model are encouraging. When school-leavers and career advisers work side by side, <u><b>they</b></u> build mutual respect and lasting life skills. Ultimately, integrating exploring career paths, developing soft skills and adapting to automation into both school curricula and family life prepares young citizens to thrive in an increasingly complex world.\n<i>(Adapted from National Educational Research Reports – Unit 9: Career Paths)</i>"
