            "passage": "<b>COMMUNITY ACTION AND PEER MENTORING IN UNIT 9: CAREER PATHS</b>\nLast year, youth unions in three provinces launched a six-month pilot programme to promote exploring career paths, developing soft skills and adapting to automation at the grassroots level. Instead of relying on formal lectures, organisers trained two hundred high school volunteers to lead weekend activities where neighbours could build both technical expertise and soft skills in the changing job market. Within the first two months, local communities reported <u><b>tangible</b></u> improvements in daily habits and neighbourhood solidarity.\nA key factor behind this success was peer-to-peer mentoring. Teenagers often feel intimidated when adults lecture them, but they respond enthusiastically when classmates demonstrate how career paths can develop soft skills like critical thinking, teamwork and adaptability. By working in small teams of five or six, participants shared practical tips, solved everyday problems together and helped one another keep up with technological changes and choose a fulfilling career.\nOrganisers also introduced simple tools to keep momentum high. <b>[I]</b> Every participating household received a visual progress board to track weekly milestones. <b>[II]</b> Local cultural centres hosted monthly gatherings where teams showcased their most creative ideas. <b>[III]</b> These celebrations turned routine tasks into a source of shared pride across the entire district. <b>[IV]</b>\nFollow-up surveys conducted six months later confirmed that over seventy-five per cent of participants continued practising career paths independently. When student volunteers take ownership of community initiatives, <u><b>their</b></u> enthusiasm inspires both younger children and older residents. The pilot programme demonstrates that youth-led action is one of the most powerful ways to bring the values of Unit 9: Career Paths to life.\n<i>(Adapted from Community Development & Youth Studies – Unit 9: Career Paths)</i>"
