            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 9: CAREER PATHS</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying exploring career paths, developing soft skills and adapting to automation have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When school-leavers and career advisers see their close friends willingly engage in career paths, they are far more likely to join in and develop soft skills like critical thinking, teamwork and adaptability.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to build both technical expertise and soft skills in the changing job market or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school keep up with technological changes and choose a fulfilling career.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 9: Career Paths)</i>"
