            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 9: CAREER PATHS</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that exploring career paths, developing soft skills and adapting to automation is important, yet still find it difficult to build both technical expertise and soft skills in the changing job market every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, school-leavers and career advisers can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on career paths. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners develop soft skills like critical thinking, teamwork and adaptability without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts keep up with technological changes and choose a fulfilling career. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 9: Career Paths report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, career paths stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 9: Career Paths)</i>"
