            "passage": "<b>SELF-REFLECTION AND LONG-TERM GROWTH IN UNIT 9: CAREER PATHS</b>\nPractising career paths is even more rewarding when learners take a few minutes each week to reflect on their progress. Educators studying exploring career paths, developing soft skills and adapting to automation have found that students who keep a brief weekly journal understand their own habits much better and find it easier to develop soft skills like critical thinking, teamwork and adaptability.\nDuring busy weeks, people sometimes miss a planned task or make mistakes. Without reflection, <u><b>they</b></u> may feel discouraged and assume that their efforts have failed. <u><b>Honest self-reflection allows learners to turn early mistakes into valuable lessons rather than reasons to give up.</b></u>\nTo make reflection effective, teachers recommend asking <u><b>constructive</b></u> questions every Sunday evening: What went well when we tried to build both technical expertise and soft skills in the changing job market this week, and what small adjustment will help us improve next week?\nOver time, this simple habit deepens personal <u><b>commitment</b></u> and helps school-leavers and career advisers keep up with technological changes and choose a fulfilling career with confidence and pride.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 9: Career Paths)</i>"
