            "passage": "<b>THE RIPPLE EFFECT OF PEER LEADERSHIP IN UNIT 10: LIFELONG LEARNING</b>\nHow do positive habits spread across a school or neighbourhood? Sociologists studying lifelong learning, adult night classes and keeping the mind sharp have discovered that peer influence is often stronger than formal rules. When adult learners and educators see their close friends willingly engage in lifelong learning courses, they are far more likely to join in and broaden horizons, boost memory and keep the brain sharp in old age.\nNevertheless, launching a new initiative is never without challenges. Organisers note that initial doubts and busy routines are common, and <u><b>they</b></u> can slow down participation during the first week. <u><b>Young people learn most effectively when they observe respected peers applying positive habits in real-life situations.</b></u>\nFor this reason, many schools encourage <u><b>voluntary</b></u> student ambassadors to lead by example. Whether demonstrating how to maintain the habit of lifelong learning in community centres and online or sharing practical tips during break time, these role models create a welcoming ripple effect that helps the whole school help working adults update professional skills and adapt to new technology.\nEven when a minor <u><b>obstacle</b></u> arises, a supportive peer group keeps members motivated. What begins as the effort of a few enthusiastic students soon grows into a proud tradition shared by the entire community.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 10: Lifelong Learning)</i>"
