            "passage": "<b>OVERCOMING BARRIERS TO DAILY PRACTICE IN UNIT 10: LIFELONG LEARNING</b>\nWhy do many high school students agree that lifelong learning, adult night classes and keeping the mind sharp is important, yet still find it difficult to maintain the habit of lifelong learning in community centres and online every day? Educational psychologists point out that modern teenagers face three main barriers: digital distractions, academic pressure and a lack of structured routines. When students feel overwhelmed, they often become <u><b>reluctant</b></u> to take on new responsibilities at home or in the community.\nFortunately, behavioural research shows that breaking large goals into micro-habits can overcome this hesitation. Instead of trying to change everything overnight, adult learners and educators can start with a fifteen-minute daily routine focused on lifelong learning courses. These manageable actions quickly build confidence and help learners broaden horizons, boost memory and keep the brain sharp in old age without interfering with their homework.\nAnother effective strategy is using a structured weekly checklist. <b>[I]</b> When teenagers write down specific times and places for their tasks, they are three times more likely to follow through. <b>[II]</b> Sharing <u><b>it</b></u> with a supportive classmate or family member adds friendly accountability. <b>[III]</b> Rather than feeling pressured, students enjoy tracking their streak of completed tasks and seeing how their efforts help working adults update professional skills and adapt to new technology. <b>[IV]</b>\nTeachers who have introduced micro-habits and checklists into their lessons on Unit 10: Lifelong Learning report remarkable improvements in student engagement. Once young people realise that small daily choices lead to meaningful results, lifelong learning courses stops feeling like a burden and becomes a natural part of who they are.\n<i>(Adapted from Adolescent Psychology & Education Review – Unit 10: Lifelong Learning)</i>"
