            "passage": "<b>COMBINING TRADITIONAL VALUES WITH MODERN TOOLS IN UNIT 10: LIFELONG LEARNING</b>\nIn the digital age, some commentators worry that modern technology might distract young people from lifelong learning, adult night classes and keeping the mind sharp. Yet recent educational projects show that when used wisely, digital tools can actually strengthen lifelong learning courses. Shared mobile calendars, reminder apps and online group checklists make it easier than ever for adult learners and educators to broaden horizons, boost memory and keep the brain sharp in old age.\nOf course, technology alone cannot solve every problem. Researchers warn against relying passively on apps while ignoring face-to-face communication, as <u><b>they</b></u> are only tools in the hands of users. <u><b>Digital tools should serve as a practical bridge to support daily cooperation rather than a replacement for genuine human interaction.</b></u>\nWhen families and classmates combine smart reminders with warm personal encouragement, they can <u><b>preserve</b></u> the core spirit of Unit 10: Lifelong Learning. For example, a quick evening message can remind everyone to maintain the habit of lifelong learning in community centres and online before gathering for a friendly conversation.\nUltimately, blending modern convenience with timeless values helps communities <u><b>enhance</b></u> their daily routines and help working adults update professional skills and adapt to new technology in a sustainable way.\n<i>(Adapted from Global Success Reading Series – Unit 10: Lifelong Learning)</i>"
