            "passage": "<b>EXPERIENTIAL LEARNING AND THE FUTURE OF UNIT 10: LIFELONG LEARNING</b>\nAcross Viet Nam, forward-thinking high schools are transforming how students learn about lifelong learning, adult night classes and keeping the mind sharp. Rather than treating Unit 10: Lifelong Learning as a set of vocabulary items to memorise for a test, teachers are integrating experiential learning into every week of the semester. Educators agree that hands-on practice is <u><b>indispensable</b></u> if young people are to broaden horizons, boost memory and keep the brain sharp in old age.\nIn these classrooms, students work in project teams to investigate real issues and propose ways to maintain the habit of lifelong learning in community centres and online. For instance, learners might interview local residents, design bilingual awareness posters or organise a school-wide campaign on lifelong learning courses. Through these authentic tasks, adult learners and educators discover how their classroom English connects directly to everyday life.\nAssessment methods are evolving alongside teaching styles. <b>[I]</b> Instead of relying solely on written quizzes, schools evaluate how effectively students collaborate and apply their plans. <b>[II]</b> Each team keeps a reflective portfolio documenting the challenges they faced and how their actions helped help working adults update professional skills and adapt to new technology. <b>[III]</b> This approach rewards creativity, persistence and genuine teamwork rather than rote memorisation. <b>[IV]</b>\nGraduates of experiential programmes consistently report higher confidence when entering university and the workplace. Students <u><b>who</b></u> have practised solving real-world problems in high school adapt much faster to new environments. By making lifelong learning, adult night classes and keeping the mind sharp an active, lived experience, education equips the next generation with both language proficiency and civic responsibility.\n<i>(Adapted from Modern Curriculum & Pedagogy Journal – Unit 10: Lifelong Learning)</i>"
